Durarara!! AU
Chapter 15 – Part 1
Curtis was nearly back in his own neighborhood when a familiar shadow fell into step beside him.
“Why, hello there.”
The voice was light, amused, and unmistakable.
Curtis didn’t break stride. He only exhaled through his nose, long black braids shifting against his back as he kept his eyes on the street ahead.
“…The next words better be an apology for stabbing me, Mr. Izaya.”
Izaya’s smile widened at once, hands tucked into his pockets as he matched Curtis’s pace with easy, unbothered precision. The streetlights caught the sharp edge of his expression.
“Apology?” He tilted his head, as if tasting the word. “That’s a strong demand from someone who grabbed me by the collar and tried to hand me to Shizu-chan like a peace offering.”
A soft, delighted click of the tongue.
“Still. You’re looking remarkably whole for a man who took a board to the head and two holes in the shoulder. I almost feel insulted. Most people stay down longer after I stick them.”
His eyes slid sideways toward Curtis, bright with interest.
“Miss me already?”
Curtis kept walking, expression flat under the streetlights.
“Yeah. I guess that was a mistake on my part. My apologies.” A short pause. “But after learning about the sheer amount of shit you caused for him, I wasn’t exactly in the wrong either. I would have understood if you stabbed me once for punching you. But twice was unnecessary.”
Izaya let out a quiet, delighted laugh — the soft kind that never reached anything like warmth.
“Unnecessary is such a subjective word.” He glanced sideways at Curtis, eyes gleaming. “One is a reply. Two is a conversation. I was simply making sure you understood the tone.”
He walked another few steps in easy silence before continuing, voice light and poisonous in equal measure.
“And as for Shizu-chan… well. You’ve been in the city for five minutes and you’re already collecting other people’s histories like souvenirs. How much did they tell you, I wonder? That I’m the villain? That he’s the poor beast I keep poking for fun?”
A thin smile.
“People love simple stories. They’re easier to carry. The truth is usually uglier and more mutual than anyone likes to admit.”
Izaya’s gaze sharpened with genuine interest.
“Still. You apologize, then immediately justify yourself, then grade my stabbing technique for excess. You’re a very strange foreigner, Curtis Nightstar.”
Curtis gave a short, dry breath that was almost a laugh.
“Hah. If that makes you a villain, then I must be the devil for what I’ve done before coming here.” He glanced sideways, mismatched eyes calm. “Anyways. How did it feel when no info came back? Only a missing kid poster.”
Izaya’s grin spread slowly, sharp and delighted, like Curtis had just handed him a gift he already suspected was coming.
“Oh?” He drew the sound out, clearly pleased. “So you knew I’d look. Of course you did.”
He tipped his head back slightly, as if savoring the rarity of the dead end.
“It felt irritating. And interesting. Do you have any idea how rarely that happens? People leave trails. School records, medical notes, immigration noise, old addresses, family complaints, something. You left a gap with a child’s face stuck to the front of it.”
His eyes gleamed under the streetlights.
“Northwest Territories. Missing. Then nothing. Until a grown version of that same face starts walking around Ikebukuro cracking pavement, collecting dangerous friends, and speaking to headless women like it’s ordinary.”
Izaya’s smile thinned into something more intent.
“So tell me, Devil. What sits in the space between the poster and you?”
Curtis didn’t answer right away.
He kept walking under the streetlights, long black braids shifting against his back, and then — slowly — a calm, enigmatic smile touched his mouth. Not warm. Not defensive. Just closed and deliberate, the kind of expression that acknowledged the question without giving it anywhere to land.
Izaya watched that smile with open interest, waiting for the rest.
It never came.
Curtis simply looked ahead again, as if the missing years between a Northwest Territories poster and the man now walking beside him were not a mystery he intended to solve for free.
The silence stretched, clean and intentional.
Whatever sat in that gap stayed his.
Curtis’s calm smile lingered as he finally spoke, voice even and unhurried.
“Oh. That kind of info will cost you a lot.” A short pause. “I don’t take money either.”
He glanced sideways at Izaya, mismatched eyes unreadable.
“And I bet I could find all the juicy info I need on you from people in the city… the yakuza.” Another beat. “So. What can you offer me?”
For the first time since falling into step beside him, Izaya’s expression flickered — not with fear, but with the sharp, delighted recalculation of someone who had expected a closed wall and instead found a counteroffer.
Then the grin returned, thinner and more interested than before.
“…You’re serious.” He let out a soft, almost appreciative breath. “Most people either run from me, try to threaten me poorly, or sell themselves too cheaply. You’re pricing your past like a professional and naming the Awakusu in the same breath.”
Izaya’s eyes gleamed under the streetlights as he studied Curtis more carefully.
“What I can offer depends on what you actually want. Protection? Names? Access? Someone removed from your path without your hands getting dirty?” A light shrug. “Or is this less about tools and more about leverage — making sure I understand you’re not another lost foreigner I can peel open for free?”
He tilted his head, smile sharp.
“Tell me the currency, Nightstar. Then I’ll decide whether you’re expensive… or worth it.”
Curtis didn’t slow down. His voice stayed calm, almost conversational, as if he were stating something obvious about the weather.
“Something you can’t offer in the slightest.” A short pause. “Friendship.”
He glanced sideways at Izaya, mismatched eyes steady.
“You don’t see anyone as friends. Everyone is a specimen for you to look at. You love humanity — not individuals.”
The words landed cleanly.
For a moment Izaya said nothing. The usual ready-made amusement stayed on his face, but something behind it went stiller, sharper, like a blade that had just been tested for balance. Then he laughed — soft, genuine in its enjoyment, and completely without warmth.
“…Oh, I do like you.”
He walked another few steps before continuing, voice light and precise.
“Most people accuse me of being cruel, or manipulative, or lonely. They’re all reaching for a moral complaint. You just described the mechanism.” His eyes slid toward Curtis, gleaming. “You’re right. I don’t collect friends. Friends require a kind of mutual blindness I find boring. People are much more interesting when you can see the gears turning.”
A thin smile.
“Humanity is vast, contradictory, and endlessly entertaining. Individuals…” He gave a small, dismissive tilt of his head. “Individuals are temporary arrangements of appetite and fear. I can enjoy them. I can use them. I can even become fond of the patterns they make. But friendship?”
Izaya’s smile widened, almost cheerful.
“You’re correct. That is the one currency I don’t carry. Which makes it a very inconvenient thing for you to ask for.”
He looked at Curtis more directly now.
“So if that’s truly the price of your past… then you’re going to remain a mystery to me for a while longer. How refreshing.”
Curtis kept his eyes on the street ahead, voice flat and final.
“Why are you like that?” A short pause. “I’m not going to play your games, Izaya. If you offer me a piece, I’ll offer you a piece. If I know you’re lying — and trust me, I will — I won’t give you anything.”
The terms landed without decoration. No room for the usual spiral of half-truths and entertainment.
Izaya’s smile didn’t vanish, but it changed shape — less theatrical, more attentive. He studied Curtis the way he studied rare outcomes: with the quiet pleasure of something that refused to behave as expected.
“…Direct trade. Verified honesty. Immediate termination if I cheat.” He let out a soft breath of amusement. “You’re trying to put rules on me.”
A few steps passed in silence before he continued, lighter again but no longer purely mocking.
“Why am I like this? Because people are at their most honest when they’re cornered, flattered, frightened, or in love — and I got tired of waiting for those moments to arrive by accident. So I learned how to arrange them.” His eyes flicked sideways. “Most find that answer repulsive. You might find it practical. That alone makes you more interesting than half this city.”
Izaya slipped one hand from his pocket and gestured loosely between them.
“Fine. Your terms. A piece for a piece. No games — or at least none you can catch.” The grin returned, sharp and delighted. “But be careful, Nightstar. Once you start trading with me, you may discover you don’t like the value of what you get back.”
He tilted his head.
“Who opens first?”
Izaya didn’t wait for an answer.
He smiled as if the question of who opened first had already been decided the moment Curtis agreed to trade at all, and spoke into the space before the foreigner could claim it.
“Then I’ll start.” His voice was light, almost generous. “A piece for a piece.”
He looked ahead down the street, hands back in his pockets, as if discussing the weather rather than blood and history.
“Shizu-chan and I have known each other since we were children. The hatred isn’t a costume. It’s real. But so is the fact that we keep finding each other in the same rooms, the same fights, the same unfinished arguments. Most people call it a feud. I call it continuity. He is one of the few people in this city who never bores me — even when he’s trying to turn me into paste against the pavement.”
A thin pause.
“There. A real piece. Not a rumor. Not a performance. The shape of something I don’t usually hand over cleanly.”
Izaya’s eyes slid back to Curtis, gleaming with expectation.
“Your turn, Nightstar. Make it equal.”
Curtis didn’t even slow down.
“Try again.” His voice was flat, unimpressed. “You two don’t just find each other. You deliberately seek him out. Testing him. Seeing if he’s something more than human.”
A short pause. He glanced sideways, mismatched eyes sharp.
“What’s the matter? Did I hit the truth almost?”
For a fraction of a second, Izaya’s smile failed to arrive on time.
It was small — barely more than a hitch in the usual ready expression — but on him it landed like a tell. Then the grin came back, thinner and brighter than before, the delight of a man who had just watched someone reach further into the mechanism than most ever managed.
“…Almost,” he said softly.
The word carried no denial.
Izaya looked ahead again, the streetlights sliding over his face as he tasted the accuracy of it.
“You really do listen. Most people stop at ‘Izaya torments Shizuo for fun.’ They never ask what I’m testing for.” A quiet, appreciative click of the tongue. “Yes. I seek him out. Yes, I push. Because ordinary human beings break in predictable ways, and he doesn’t. He bends the street. He stands back up. He remains… incomplete as an explanation.”
His eyes gleamed.
“So I keep pressing the question. Is he still human? Was he ever only human? Or is there something in him the rest of us only brush against when we’re desperate enough?”
Izaya’s smile widened, sharper now.
“There. A cleaner piece. Uncomfortable. Accurate. And more than I usually give strangers on a sidewalk.”
He turned his head toward Curtis fully.
“Now. Your turn. And don’t insult the trade by offering me something smaller than what you just forced out of me.”
Curtis shook his head once, calm and final.
“No. The terms were you don’t lie. You lied the first time. You get nothing.” A short, dry pause. The corner of his mouth lifted just enough to mock. “Try again on our gameshow.”
The jab was light, but it landed cleanly against Izaya’s usual control of the tempo.
For a moment Izaya simply stared at him. Then he laughed — short, sharp, and genuinely irritated beneath the amusement.
“…You’re enforcing the rules like a hall monitor.” His smile remained, but it had tightened at the edges. “Most people are so busy being clever around me that they forget they can simply refuse to pay. You’re actually doing it.”
He exhaled through his nose, recalculating in real time.
“Fine. First answer void. No trade completed. No debt incurred.” One hand slipped free of his pocket as he gestured lightly, almost theatrical in surrender. “How principled of you. And how annoying.”
His eyes narrowed with renewed interest.
“Very well, Nightstar. New piece. Clean. No soft framing this time.”
The playfulness thinned into something more precise.
“I don’t only test Shizu-chan because he’s strong. I test him because if he is something more than human, then the rest of this city’s violence is just children playing with sticks. And I refuse to be the only one who notices.”
Izaya’s smile returned, sharper than before.
“There. No costume. Your move — if you still feel like rewarding honesty.”
Curtis delivered it flat, almost offhand, like he was commenting on the weather.
“So you’re a virgin with an obsession with another man. Some might call that gay, Izaya.”
Izaya stopped walking for half a step.
Then he laughed — bright, delighted, and completely unbothered. The sound carried down the quiet stretch of street like he’d just been handed an unexpected gift.
“Oh, that’s crude.” His grin spread wide, eyes gleaming under the streetlights. “And lazy. I expected better phrasing from someone who trades information like a professional.”
He started walking again, falling back into pace beside Curtis with theatrical ease.
“For the record, Nightstar, fixation and desire aren’t the same currency. I can spend years pressing on a single person without wanting them in my bed. Shizu-chan is many things to me — an unfinished question, a rare specimen, a source of endless entertainment — but the conclusion you’re reaching is the kind of simple little story people tell when they’re trying to wound instead of understand.”
Izaya tilted his head, smile sharpening.
“Also. Whether I am or am not a virgin is spectacularly irrelevant to the trade. But thank you for revealing that when you can’t control the board, you go for the cheap shot.”
His eyes flicked sideways, bright with amusement.
“Your move still stands. Or are you conceding the piece because you ran out of actual substance?”
Curtis actually laughed — short, genuine, and unbothered by the earlier jab landing soft.
“Calling him Shizu-chan isn’t helping your case, even if it pisses him off.” He shook his head once, still faintly amused, then let the humor thin into something more deliberate.
“But here’s a little piece of me.” His voice settled back into that flat, even register. “I traveled by foot across the USA. Got deported to Mexico for being mistaken for Mexican. Came back to the States sometime after. Here I am.”
A short pause.
“How’s that?”
Izaya’s expression brightened immediately, the earlier mock-offense forgotten the moment real material hit the table. He tasted the details like wine.
“…On foot. Across the country. Then deported by racial misread, then back again, then Japan.” His smile widened, sharp with pleasure. “That’s not a little piece, Nightstar. That’s a migration pattern with teeth. No clean paperwork. No soft landings. Just movement and stubborn survival.”
He glanced sideways, eyes gleaming.
“It also explains the missing records better than most lies would. A boy who becomes a gap tends to stay difficult to file.” A soft, appreciative click of the tongue. “Accepted. Equal enough for now.”
Izaya slipped both hands back into his pockets, clearly already turning the information over for later use.
“Your border-hopping past buys you one more honest piece from me. Or…” His smile thinned into something more dangerous. “We can stop here and pretend this was a friendly sidewalk chat. Your choice.”
Curtis gave a small shrug, the earlier sharpness cooling back into something almost casual.
“Technically, this is friendly. No one’s getting stabbed.” A short pause. “You’re a thrill seeker of a different sort.”
He looked ahead down the street, long black braids shifting against his back.
“But I’m afraid the games stop now, Izaya. Unless you can meet me on the friends part… then I’m afraid I’m not going to tell you anything else.”
In his mind, the rest of the map stayed sealed — Italy, Russia, Europe, all the roads between the deportation and the day he stepped into Ikebukuro. None of it was going on the table.
Izaya slowed, then stopped walking entirely.
For a moment he simply studied Curtis under the streetlights, the usual bright amusement thinning into something more focused and faintly annoyed. Being refused was not new. Being refused cleanly, after a successful partial trade, was rarer.
“…You’re really going to sit on the rest of it for something I already told you I don’t offer.” His smile returned, but it was tighter, more thoughtful. “Friendship as the gate. How sentimental. And how effective.”
He exhaled through his nose, almost laughing at himself.
“Fine. You’ve closed the shop. I got a migration pattern, a missing-child silhouette, and confirmation that you know how to enforce terms. You got a cleaner read on me and Shizu-chan than most people ever dig up.”
Izaya tilted his head, eyes gleaming with unfinished hunger.
“But don’t mistake the end of this sidewalk for the end of my interest, Nightstar. You’ve made yourself expensive. That only makes the eventual purchase more entertaining.”
He took one step back, already angling away into the darker stretch of street.
“Until next time. Try not to get stabbed by anyone less charming than me.”
Curtis stopped as well, turning just enough to face him fully under the streetlights. The earlier sparring edge was gone. What remained was plain and deliberate.
“Before we part.” His voice stayed even. “I sincerely do want nothing from you. And though you stabbed me, I hold no resentment. I hope one day we can be friends.”
A short pause.
“I couldn’t care less what people say about you. But don’t take that for weakness. I’m still not letting my guard down when it comes to my info.”
The words hung between them — open without being soft, generous without being naive.
Izaya studied him in silence for a longer beat than usual. The sharp little smile remained, but something behind it had gone quieter, more assessing, like a man turning an unfamiliar object over in his hands and finding no immediate place to set it down.
“…You’re a strange one,” he said at last. Not mocking. Almost observational. “Most people either want me useful, want me gone, or want the version of me that makes their own story simpler. You’re standing there offering friendship after two stab wounds and a refused information trade, while simultaneously locking the door on your past.”
A thin breath of laughter left him.
“I don’t know what to do with that yet.” His eyes gleamed under the streetlights. “Which means I’ll be thinking about it. Congratulations, Nightstar. You’ve managed to become unfinished business without giving me the rest of the map.”
Izaya took another step back, hands in his pockets, the night already starting to claim him.
“Keep your guard up. I’d be disappointed if you made it easy later.”
Then, lighter, almost careless over his shoulder:
“And for what it’s worth… I don’t resent you either.”
He disappeared into the darker stretch of street, leaving Curtis alone with the quiet and the unspent remainder of his own history.
Curtis disappeared into the quieter stretch leading toward his neighborhood, long black braids fading under the spaced streetlights. Izaya walked the other way, hands deep in his pockets, the night air cool against the residual sharpness of the exchange.
For a while he said nothing at all.
Then, alone with the dark, the frustration arrived properly.
He closed it.
He actually closed the trade and walked away with the rest of the map still in his pocket.
Izaya’s mouth twitched. Most people eventually opened — from fear, from vanity, from the need to be understood by someone dangerous enough to matter. Curtis had done the opposite. He had looked straight through the performance, named the mechanism underneath, rejected the first dishonest piece, accepted only what was real, and then set a price Izaya did not carry.
Friendship.
The word irritated him more than the refusal itself.
Not because it was sentimental. Because it was structurally unavailable. He had spent years learning people as systems — appetite, fear, contradiction, leverage. He could mimic warmth. He could manufacture loyalty in others. He could even become fond of certain patterns. But the thing Curtis had named, the mutual and unarmored version of it, was not a tool Izaya had ever built for himself. He had never needed to.
And that was the real problem.
Curtis Nightstar had seen through him more cleanly than almost anyone in Ikebukuro. Not with moral outrage. Not with wounded idealism. With calm, practical accuracy. As if Izaya were simply another readable surface. That alone was rare enough to be insulting. Combined with a past that began at a missing-child poster and refused to continue on paper, it became something closer to a challenge.
Izaya laughed under his breath, quiet and sharp in the empty street.
Fine.
Stay expensive.
Keep the forest and the deportation and whatever else you’re sitting on.
Set a price I don’t know how to pay yet.
His smile thinned into something hungrier.
That only means I’ll have to learn a new currency.
Or find another way to open you.
Either option, he had to admit, sounded entertaining.
